1,"ve_over_need":0.7508343535354023,"refire_band":[0.42882646960003007,0.19058954204445783],"engine":{"jet_share":0.1383321103433282,"exhaust_m_s":14416899.625808815,"thrust_N":171369.47221213387,"heat_W":6083428780923.341,"heat_over":2252.887001285098,"xray_heat_share":0.5228813529037263,"hit_heat_share":0.4505581607560769,"fast_impulse_share":0.0,"deposited_kg_s":0.026150757004076958}},{"material":"W","sigma_g_cm2":1.0,"absorbed":0.6916696995666425,"tamper_kg":2.0576183199579246e-07,"tamper_over_fuel":0.031023784901758014,"xray_left_share":0.43720389744880234,"jet_J":278841912.3804646,"exhaust_m_s":16175368.693796532,"impulse_ratio":1.9013077917725296,"ve_over_need":0.8424156935005467,"refire_band":[0.6225027296099782,0.276667879826657],"engine":{"jet_share":0.17789218316288144,"exhaust_m_s":16175368.693796529,"thrust_N":196419.68398267758,"heat_W":6148320711666.183,"heat_over":2276.9185454230487,"xray_heat_share":0.4004268458584539,"hit_heat_share":0.5732929982071483,"fast_impulse_share":0.0,"deposited_kg_s":0.0267148967632259}},{"material":"W","sigma_g_cm2":3.0,"absorbed":0.8330234589347423,"tamper_kg":6.172854959873774e-07,"tamper_over_fuel":0.09307135470527406,"xray_left_share":0.35333869252081657,"jet_J":319573538.8662494,"exhaust_m_s":16816636.541891154,"impulse_ratio":2.095946717840544,"ve_over_need":0.8758130218210698,"refire_band":[0.7497211130412681,0.33320938357389696],"engine":{"jet_share":0.20387765248302003,"exhaust_m_s":16816636.541891154,"thrust_N":216527.3785466207,"heat_W":6190945690723.581,"heat_over":2292.703929736184,"xray_heat_share":0.32138816363704276,"hit_heat_share":0.652512620651958,"fast_impulse_share":0.0,"deposited_kg_s":0.028326724646508594}}],"audit":{"geometry_sky":0.6305845688364754,"ledger_pickup":0.6305845688364754,"pickup_terms":{"piped_W":44001050739483.44,"charged_hits_W":40518077423011.69,"neutral_W":6380890702665.633,"sky_total_compact":0.9790915824543969,"per_point_hit_share":0.09236231756475953},"geometry_terms":{"shield":0.07209202003949478,"coils_and_loops":0.37218854171564913,"cage_lines_optics":0.18630400708133152},"findings":["The 54.6 % is not a geometric quantity. The ledger derives it as (piped heat - hit share x charged power) / neutral power from the compact study: (44.0 TW - 40.5 TW) / 6.38 TW. It is the small difference of two large numbers: one point of hit share moves it by 0.09.","It also comes from a different source state: the steady-plasma spectrum (STEADY, with synchrotron), not the target burn (COUPLED).","The compact study's own sky ledger covered 97.9% of the burn (with the radiator blades and the neighbour engines), and its piped heat leaves out what edge-on fins and the plug re-radiate themselves.","The geometric trace of every drawn part gives 63.1%: shield 7.2%, coils and loops 37.2%, cage, lines and optics 18.6%. The coils, loops and shield alone give 44.4%, close to the ledger figure, which is why the two looked related.","Decision: the COUPLED ledger now uses the geometric trace (63.1 %) for the X-ray and neutron pickup of the compact engine; the old figure is kept and labelled as the compact study's piped share."],"residual":{"share":0.00015168299350102178,"finding":"The 0.25 % energy residual of the burn was the thermal energy of ions created or lost at fixed temperature (D-D-n and D-T turn two ions into one). The ledger now books it as its own term (number change); about 0.02 % remains, the time-step error of the integration."}},"tech":[{"lever":"Spin-polarised fuel","physics":"x1.5 D-3He reactivity (established); survival through compression is unknown","enabler":"cryogenic polarised fuel, nanoscale shells that keep spins aligned, AI-shaped compression","status":"SPECULATIVE"},{"lever":"Higher areal density","physics":"more burn-up, but the burning electrons radiate more: the X-ray share grows","enabler":"precision target manufacture, pulse shaping by learned controllers","status":"ESTABLISHED physics, ADVANCED manufacture"},{"lever":"Tamped, longer burn","physics":"more burn-up at the same yield; a heavy tamper mixes and raises Zeff","enabler":"graded nanostructured tampers, magnetised liners (superconducting drivers)","status":"ESTABLISHED"},{"lever":"X-ray trapping tamper","physics":"an opaque shell turns X-rays into plasma the nozzle can direct; adds exhaust mass","enabler":"graded high-Z/low-Z shells made atom by atom","status":"ESTABLISHED (cold opacity upper bound)"},{"lever":"Early extraction of protons","physics":"directed 14.7 MeV protons, but the burn loses its self-heating","enabler":"stronger fields from advanced superconductors at the target","status":"MODEL-BASED"},{"lever":"Hot-ion burn","physics":"the products heat electrons first above Ec; Te follows Ti within picoseconds at target density","enabler":"none known: the coupling is collisional, not a technology limit","status":"LIMITED BY PHYSICS"}],"findings":["At 70 keV the 14.7 MeV protons give 10% of their energy to ions and the 3.6 MeV alphas 69% (critical energies 0.7 and 3.0 MeV); the rest heats electrons, which radiate. At the design density the products stop within 7.2 um (protons) of a 40 um target, so they all deposit: the target is its own X-ray lamp.","The design burn sends 85% of its fusion energy out as X-rays and leaves 16% as plasma the nozzle can use.","Hot ions at the start change almost nothing (X-ray share 84%): ions and electrons equilibrate in picoseconds at this density. This is a physics limit, not a technology one.","Spin polarisation raises the yield x1.11 and burn-up to 75%, but the X-ray share stays 83%: it helps the fuel, not the radiation.","3He-rich fuel (D:3He 1:3) cuts the neutron share to 0.5% (from 2.9%) at X-ray share 99%.","Early extraction is the only lever that lowers the X-ray share at the source: 20% out gives X-rays 71%, extractable 30%, yield x0.97; 50% out gives X-rays 49%, extractable 53%, yield x0.86; 80% out gives X-rays 41%, extractable 79%, yield x0.18. The burn loses its self-heating, so the yield falls.","A tungsten tamper of 1 g/cm2 round the fuel (3.1% of its mass) would absorb 69% of the X-rays (cold opacity, an upper bound) and leave 44% of the fusion energy as X-rays. The absorbed energy becomes plasma the nozzle can direct, so the exhaust speeds up to 16,175 km/s (0.84 of the mission's) and the impulse per pulse grows x1.90. Light tampers (LiH, carbon) absorb too little.","Density and burn time decide the split: x0.3 areal density gives X-rays 53% and extractable 50% (yield x0.72); a faster disassembly gives 67% and 34%. A denser or longer burn raises burn-up a little and sends almost everything out as X-rays.","The fuel-side levers together (polarised, 3He-rich, tamped, x3 areal density) are the worst case: yield x0.60, X-rays 105% of the fusion energy (the driver heat radiates too).","The source-side levers together (polarised, x0.3 areal density, 20 % early extraction): yield x0.86, burn-up 58%, X-rays 45%, extractable 59%. With a tungsten tamper on top, the X-rays leaving the target fall to 23% of the fusion energy and the thrust grows x2.1, but the engine heat stays x2,011 its radiator share (design x2,200): the plasma that no longer radiates strikes the hardware instead (78% of the heat). The next lever is the nozzle's hit share, not the fuel.","Adding cold propellant lowers the exhaust speed: the design is short of energy per kilogram (coupled exhaust 8,772 km/s against the mission's 19,201 km/s), not short of mass."],"provenance":{"study":"lab/studies/vertex-compact-engine/vertex-source (source.py)","branch_head":"c169a9f","note":"Copied once by lab/studies/vertex-compact-engine/vertex-thermal/export.py."}}